{#
A per-user fan-out of one layer-2 presentation: `mount --bind` from the
node the group grant actually resolved to (entry.symlink_target, still
named for the symlink this used to be -- see plan_mounts() for why a
real symlink can't work here) onto each member's own path inside their
container.

Binding the *presented* path, not the transport underneath it, is
deliberate: the presentation is where the group ownership lives, and a
bind of it is the same filesystem at a second location, so each member
sees the grant exactly as the shared mount presents it.
#}
[Unit]
Description=stortree per-user bind mount %i (managed by stortree, do not edit by hand)
{#
PartOf= on every mount this bind depends on, for the same reason as
stortree-mount@.service.j2 -- and a bind mount is the worst-affected of
the three: `Type=oneshot` + `RemainAfterExit=yes` means the unit reports
active forever once its ExecStart succeeded, with nothing left running
to notice its mount went away. Both edges below can take it out. The
*source* is the presentation this binds from, so remounting it leaves
this bind pointing at a detached tree; the *container* is the
presentation this bind's own mountpoint lives inside, so remounting that
detaches the bind outright. Either way the path reverts to the empty
directory underneath, silently, until something restarts this unit --
which nothing ever did, because as far as systemd was concerned it never
stopped.
#}
{#
Only when the source actually is a presentation. A `group`-only node
collapses to one shared mount at `.mounts/<name>` plus a bind per
member -- but on the host that owns the subtree, with no `rclone`
anywhere above it, that shared path is an ordinary local directory whose
grant is a real chown rather than a bindfs mount, so there is no
`stortree-mount@` unit to name. Naming one regardless is not a
dependency that goes unmet later, it is a unit systemd refuses to start
at all ("Unit stortree-mount@... not found") -- which took out both
members' folders the first time a node like that reached a fleet.
`symlink_target_slug` is None in exactly that case
(_relate_plan_entries()); the `requires_slug` edge below has always been
guarded the same way, for the same reason.

RequiresMountsFor stays either way: the bind's source has to be on a
mounted filesystem whether or not a presentation is what mounts it, and
for a plain local directory this resolves to whatever real mount
contains it.
#}
{% if entry.symlink_target_slug %}
After=stortree-mount@{{ entry.symlink_target_slug }}.service
Requires=stortree-mount@{{ entry.symlink_target_slug }}.service
PartOf=stortree-mount@{{ entry.symlink_target_slug }}.service
{% endif %}
RequiresMountsFor={{ stortree_root }}/{{ entry.symlink_target }}
{% if entry.requires_slug %}
After=stortree-mount@{{ entry.requires_slug }}.service
Requires=stortree-mount@{{ entry.requires_slug }}.service
PartOf=stortree-mount@{{ entry.requires_slug }}.service
RequiresMountsFor={{ stortree_root }}/{{ entry.requires_slug_path }}
{% endif %}

[Service]
Type=oneshot
RemainAfterExit=yes
ExecStart=/bin/mount --bind {{ stortree_root }}/{{ entry.symlink_target }} {{ stortree_root }}/{{ entry.local_path }}
{#
Tolerant stop, matching the rclone units' own: with PartOf= above, this
unit is now routinely stopped as part of a parent remount, by which
point its mountpoint can already be gone along with the tree it lived
in. A bare umount fails there and leaves the unit failed rather than
cleanly stopped -- nothing to unmount is a successful stop, not an
error.
#}
{#
`-l` (lazy), matching the `fusermount -uz` the other two layers have
always used, and for a stronger reason than symmetry: a plain `umount`
here is what turned an ordinary reboot into a seven-and-a-half-minute
one in production.

A bind's source is a FUSE mount. When it goes away underneath -- which
at shutdown it routinely does, since the layer below is being torn down
in the same pass -- a blocking `umount` enters uninterruptible sleep in
the kernel waiting for a daemon that is already gone, and a process in D
state cannot be killed by anything, SIGKILL included ("Processes still
around after final SIGKILL. Entering failed mode."). `umount -l`
detaches the mountpoint immediately and lets the kernel clean up the
reference behind it, so there is nothing to block on.
#}
ExecStop=-/bin/sh -c 'mountpoint -q "{{ stortree_root }}/{{ entry.local_path }}" || exit 0; /bin/umount -l "{{ stortree_root }}/{{ entry.local_path }}"'
{#
See stortree_mounts_stop_timeout in the role's defaults for why this is
capped at all, and why the transports keep systemd's own 90s.
#}
TimeoutStopSec={{ stortree_mounts_stop_timeout }}

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
